# Mutation cases for W3C SCXML 6.5.2 — the clause an EMPTY `<finalize>`
# carries, and the line it draws against an absent one.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/empty_finalize_is_not_an_absent_one.cases`.
#
# "If no executable content is specified, the SCXML Processor MUST update the
# data model each time an event is received from the child process ... for
# each item in the 'namelist' attribute and each such <param> element, the
# Processor MUST update the corresponding location as if by <assign> with any
# return value that has a name that matches ... Note that the automatic update
# does not take place if the <finalize> element is absent as opposed to
# empty."
#
# The whole corpus holds two `<finalize>` documents (W3C 233/234) and zero
# empty ones, so nothing measured this until the fixture below. Nothing
# implemented it either: `finalize_content` is one string, so an empty element
# and a missing one were the same value and every engine gates on that string
# being non-empty.
#
# The synthesis is in the parser rather than in seven templates because the
# clause names the content the empty element stands for. That decides which
# suite these cases can be measured against, and the first attempt got it
# wrong: a `-p sce-rust-tests` round with `mutation_regen` SURVIVED two of
# three cases, because the regeneration resolves `sce-codegen` through
# `sce_codegen_require`, which keeps an existing binary whose
# `verify-generator` check passes — and that check is over the TEMPLATE tree.
# A mutation to `parser.rs` changes no template, so the stale binary generated
# the unmutated tree and the round measured nothing. The bridge carries a
# mutated TEMPLATE into a generated tree; it does not carry a mutated
# COMPILER.
#
# So the selector is `-p sce-build`: the parser is linked into that crate's
# own test binaries, the mutation reaches the code with no bridge at all, and
# the oracle is the parser-level witness. The seven-channel behaviour keeps
# its own witness in `integration_resources/empty_finalize_updates_the_location/`.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --features cli --test empty_finalize_is_not_an_absent_one
mutation_targets sce-build/src/parser.rs

mutation_oracles sce-build/tests/empty_finalize_is_not_an_absent_one.rs

# ── The empty element goes back to meaning nothing ──────────────────
#
# The state this clause was in before the round: an empty `<finalize>` and a
# missing one produce the same empty string, so the automatic update never
# happens. `failNotUpdated` is what a document sees.

mutation_case "an empty finalize stands for no content at all" <<'PY'
# The synthesised text is still computed and then dropped, so the helper stays
# used: `warnings = deny` turns an unused function into a compile error, and a
# mutation that will not compile is INCONCLUSIVE rather than a verdict.
edit("sce-build/src/parser.rs",
     """            if finalize_content.trim().is_empty() {
                finalize_content = synthesize_automatic_finalize(&namelist, &hybrid_params);
            }""",
     """            if finalize_content.trim().is_empty() {
                let _ = synthesize_automatic_finalize(&namelist, &hybrid_params);
            }""")
PY

# ── The update stops being conditional ──────────────────────────────
#
# "With ANY return value that has a name that matches" is a condition. Without
# the guard, an event that mentions nothing writes `undefined` over the
# parent's value — the opposite of what the clause is for, and invisible to
# any fixture whose child always answers with every name.

mutation_case "the automatic update writes even when the name is absent" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/parser.rs",
     """            "if (_event.data && _event.data.{item} !== undefined) {{ {item} = _event.data.{item}; }}\"""",
     """            "{item} = _event.data.{item};\"""")
PY

# ── The guard is inverted ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The nearest wrong answer that still compiles and still looks conditional:
# the location is written only when the event does NOT carry the name. A
# fixture that checks the value arrived is what separates this from the real
# clause; one that only checks "something ran" cannot.

mutation_case "the automatic update runs only when the name is missing" <<'PY'
edit("sce-build/src/parser.rs",
     """            "if (_event.data && _event.data.{item} !== undefined) {{ {item} = _event.data.{item}; }}\"""",
     """            "if (_event.data && _event.data.{item} === undefined) {{ {item} = _event.data.{item}; }}\"""")
PY
